 "understand": [
  {
   "p": "Many letters share the <strong>same body</strong> and differ only in their dots. Learn them as families: once you know the shape, you only need to count dots and see whether they sit above or below."
  },
  {
   "table": {
    "headers": [
     "Family",
     "Letters",
     "How to tell them apart"
    ],
    "ar": [
     1
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "boat",
      "ب ت ث",
      "1 dot below · 2 above · 3 above"
     ],
     [
      "hook",
      "ج ح خ",
      "dot inside · no dot · dot above"
     ],
     [
      "corner",
      "د ذ",
      "no dot · 1 dot above"
     ],
     [
      "slide",
      "ر ز",
      "no dot · 1 dot above"
     ],
     [
      "teeth",
      "س ش",
      "no dots · 3 dots above"
     ],
     [
      "loop",
      "ص ض",
      "no dot · 1 dot above"
     ],
     [
      "flag",
      "ط ظ",
      "no dot · 1 dot above"
     ],
     [
      "eye",
      "ع غ",
      "no dot · 1 dot above"
     ],
     [
      "round head",
      "ف ق",
      "1 dot · 2 dots (and ق dips lower)"
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "p": "Then the letters with their own shape: <span class='ar'>ا ك ل م ن ه و ي</span>. Notice that <span class='ar'>ن</span> looks like a deeper boat with one dot above, and <span class='ar'>ي</span> has two dots below."
  },
  {
   "tip": "The “heavy” letters <span class='ar'>ص ض ط ظ</span> and <span class='ar'>ق</span> are said with the back of the tongue raised. They make the vowels next to them sound deeper and darker. Compare <span class='ar'>س</span> s and <span class='ar'>ص</span> ṣ: same place in the mouth, but ṣ is fuller."
  },
  {
